da012d4e77403e276074a6d99cef7b252dff"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-16-12","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":16,"verse":12,"english":"And seeing that one having the face of a monkey, the Raksasas, deriding him, burst out into laughter, and seemed as if a mass of clouds were sending roars.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.322,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-16-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7016012","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9c5b7e4bfacf69636117c92f402671a5c7f17268e31b4672cefb4688f10875c4","compact":"dcc176034d972869b5b057e96b847727a0ddd49957d92319f504b67626dc376e","nasal":"dcc176034d972869b5b057e96b847727a0ddd49957d92319f504b67626dc376e"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-16-13","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":16,"verse":13,"english":"Thereat, growing enraged, the reverend Nandi Sankara's other body spoke into that Rākṣasa The Ten-necked one present there.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.385,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-16-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7016013","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b5ea297de1b247baeb277409673151d48d7ec756d1d0553b48abb06b01eca885","compact":"7f76c3babcb729e0a82f01ac3d3734d6a1d68bd73c19494ffc03c8bbd42b975d","nasal":"9c1d82087f2f79e3338a6c9b8078643afb60e54e52705e4ec4e19a862a8670da"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-16-14","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":16,"verse":14,"english":"Having entered there without any councillor the vicious Rāvana, the lord of Räkşasas, on beholding that chaste damsel with sweet smiles seated on a royal seat, being possessed by desire, grew anxious to hold her by the hand, as one, under the influence of death, catches a sleeping serpent. Beholding that lord of Rākşasas with loose cloth and understanding him as desirous of holding (her) the deity, having huge arms, asleep and enveloped with flames, laughed aloud. And searched suddenly by his energy, Ravana, the dread of all people, fell down on earth like to an up-rooted tree. Beholding that Raksasa fallen he said the following words: “Rise up, O foremost of Raksasas, you shall not meet with death today; you shall live and be protected, O Raksasa, by the great Patriarch's boon. Do you therefore go away, O Ravana, with confidence, you shall not experience death just now.\"","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.127,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-23-113"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7016014","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a3b958d6498ad4c299769eb73ba28941641f416f957a57293420ba8f4effc3d5","compact":"8d74114e5fd77793936aa90ba5ee75fc6b9d1c922be251e7b9e16489175d320d","nasal":"906f461172a5d0676cf35f6f72cce963f7865fe12bc478f802504d8e78a3bf44"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-16-15","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":16,"verse":15,"english":"“As O Daśānana, deriding me for my monkery-form, you have indulged in a laughter resembling the bursting of thunder, do monkeys endowed with prowess, and possessed of my form and energy shall be born for compassing the destruction of your race.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.4,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-16-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7016015","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"844b24d2e73fa7aac0d89a8c15de5c03a04825d8568b94825b2f4a3696484461","compact":"7752aeaae8bd082a47512c9de0577ee0ea2f7e7081a6893c6ea80f473d87b4cb","nasal":"52094cda738c27718d73bfd6bd987000949651f7eab418c6127b6c475278073a"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-16-16","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":16,"verse":16,"english":"But, O night-ranger, I can even now end you quite; yet I must not slay you for you have ere this been slain by your acts.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.586,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-16-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7016016","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5a2730bd3fa9e1aa15475f1731af4228e4a8d9fbdc351181decdac59fbeae152","compact":"ca6600bb85af8cfec856f842da7110e3dd3e662d12b2bd4e127c461f0e9dd1a8","nasal":"748a67cde57cbb95a373c088a93543b04f23fa3bf1824c6d79d7631c40747c1d"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-16-17","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":16,"verse":17,"english":"But without heeding Nandi's speech, that highly powerful one Daśānana coming to the mountain, said.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.491,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-16-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7016017","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0a6eb505ad9bd1a386773bf386cfb9d3073fe3c47e82acb5e7b30c8e36cbe606","compact":"6e401b3e9cece1e03043f32b8e4a829dd441d0ef577e857f70639a5c40f77299","nasal":"9b1468e1edec2a3e63300ea8888e031c72138d4a6142c743a1833a72838fbfde"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-16-18","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":16,"verse":18,"english":"'O Gopati, I will even uproot this mountain, for whom Pușpaka was deprived of its motion as I was journeying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.772,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-16-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7016018","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e38d3ccf70e6addd87876bf8734a69d23e792ac636308f6d1f166a6aed43a08f","compact":"b03589a8c435d548da9502d83cc47a54916c899f196aba972ee4775a9701fbc0","nasal":"9f50e79952dd3115d9c2c6d20047143cee1e9ac625727aae987a7bc68c30a9db"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-16-19","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":16,"verse":19,"english":"It must be known by what influence Bhavasvara sport here king-like. He does not know that an occasion of fear has presented itself?'"